latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Initialize:\n   a. Create a deep copy of the input grid to work on.\n   b. Define constants for colors (BLACK = 0, SKY_BLUE = 8).\n\n2. Analyze the grid:\n   a. Iterate through the grid to identify all non-black shapes.\n   b. For each shape, store its color, coordinates, and a unique identifier.\n\n3. Create a graph representation:\n   a. Create nodes for each identified shape.\n   b. Calculate distances between all pairs of shapes.\n\n4. Generate a minimum spanning tree:\n   a. Use Prim's algorithm to create a minimum spanning tree of the shape graph.\n   b. This tree represents the optimal connections between shapes.\n\n5. Create the sky blue network:\n   a. For each edge in the minimum spanning tree:\n      i. Use A* pathfinding to find the shortest path between the two shapes.\n      ii. Expand the path to be 3 cells wide:\n          - For each cell in the path, color it and its 8 neighbors sky blue.\n          - If near a grid edge, adjust to ensure the path stays within bounds.\n      iii. Optimize the path:\n           - Straighten unnecessary bends where possible.\n           - Ensure consistent 3-cell width throughout.\n\n6. Post-processing:\n   a. Fill in any 1 or 2-cell gaps between sky blue areas to ensure continuity.\n   b. If any shapes are near the grid edge, create a 3-cell wide sky blue border.\n\n7. Restore original shapes:\n   a. Iterate through the original shape coordinates.\n   b. Overwrite any sky blue cells with the original shape colors.\n\n8. Validate the solution:\n   a. Ensure all non-black shapes are connected by the sky blue network.\n   b. Verify that the sky blue network is continuous and 3 cells wide.\n   c. Check that no original shapes have been altered.\n\n9. Handle edge cases:\n   a. If the input grid is all black, return it unchanged.\n   b. If there's only one shape, create a sky blue border around it.\n\n10. Optimize (if needed):\n    a. Look for areas where the sky blue network can be reduced without breaking connectivity.\n    b. Smooth out unnecessary turns or zigzags in the network.\n\n11. Return the modified grid as the solution.\n\nThis plan addresses the core requirements of the challenge while incorporating the insights gained from the reflection. It should be adaptable to various grid sizes and shape configurations, handle edge cases, and produce efficient connections between shapes."
